Under 42 CFR Part 2, there are limited exceptions where written consent is not required for the disclosure or use of substance use disorder (SUD) treatment records. Here are the key exceptions:

1. Medical Emergencies (42 CFR § 2.51)
• Disclosure is allowed without consent if there is a bona fide medical emergency where the patient’s health is at immediate risk, and obtaining consent is impractical.
• The provider must document the emergency situation in the patient’s records.

2. Court Orders (42 CFR § 2.61 - § 2.67)
• A judge can order disclosure without consent, but the order must follow strict legal procedures to protect patient privacy.
• The court must find good cause, balancing the public interest against potential harm to the patient.

3. Crimes on Program Premises or Against Program Personnel (42 CFR § 2.12(c)(5))
• If a patient commits or threatens to commit a crime on the premises of a treatment program or against program staff, information may be disclosed to law enforcement without consent.
• This applies only to information related to the crime itself, not general treatment records.

4. Reports of Child Abuse or Neglect (42 CFR § 2.12(c)(6))
• Programs must report suspected child abuse or neglect as required by state law.
• However, these reports cannot include the patient’s substance use treatment records unless separately authorized.

5. Research Purposes (42 CFR § 2.52)
• Identifiable patient information can be disclosed without consent for research, but only if strict safeguards are followed (e.g., Institutional Review Board (IRB) approval).

6. Qualified Service Organizations (QSOs) (42 CFR § 2.12(c)(4))
• A treatment program can disclose information without consent to a Qualified Service Organization (QSO) that provides essential services (e.g., lab testing, billing).
• The QSO must have a formal written agreement with the treatment provider, ensuring confidentiality.

7. Audits and Evaluations (42 CFR § 2.53)
• Government agencies and funders can access de-identified treatment data to audit or evaluate program effectiveness.
• Patients’ personally identifiable information cannot be used for law enforcement or criminal proceedings.
